[{:seon.task/name "a-point-is-found-without-walking-the-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.ctx.wire/at-t` finds branch db's commit holding t by walking the line back from db, a ``datahike.api/commit-as-db`` a commit, on every call; `seon.system/back-point` calls it and keeps the commit when `(= t (:max-tx at))`. Measured 2026-11-11 (LMDB, Seon's layout, one-datom commits, the point at the first of n): 379k, 4.17M and 42.1M engine ticks, 1.7, 18 and 186 MB, at n = 10, 100 and 1,000 (42k ticks and 185 KB a commit). What replaces it: ``datahike.versioning/commit-at-tx`` (Datahike pin `f47067b5`, docs/storage.md#a-points-commit): `(datahike.versioning/commit-at-tx db t)` answers `{:branch :commit :t}`, the commit of db's lineage whose last transaction t' is the greatest <= t, exact when `(= t (:t p))`; `(datahike.versioning/commit-holding-tx db t)` the commit whose span holds t, which is what at-t answers now (its earliest commit holding t). Both read each commit once per process and keep it in memory, then confirm the answer with one ``konserve.core/exists?`` (9.0k ticks): measured over the same span, a first lookup 169k, 1.69M and 16.9M ticks (one commit-record read, 16.8k ticks and 64 KB, a commit walked, plus the exists?), a repeat 9.4k (the exists? alone), k reads and the exists? after k new commits. What bounds the first walk: it stops at the first commit whose last transaction is <= t, so a resume from a recent point reads few commits and one from an old point many (a 1,000-commit-old point: 1,000 record reads). The commit graph record would read cheaper (12.6k ticks, 40 KB) but holds no max-tx; carrying the walk on it would store a derivable value, not done (owner, 2026-11-11), the number recorded for him. Change: back-point takes `(datahike.versioning/commit-at-tx db t)` and keeps `:commit` when `(= t (:t p))`, else refuses as now; at-t becomes `(d/commit-as-db (:store db) (:commit (datahike.versioning/commit-holding-tx db t)))`, and where a sweep took the commit the refusal `{:type :commit-expired :branch :tx}` (ex-info at its named edge) gives `(d/as-of db t)` as now; `:tx-after-head` and `:tx-before-first-commit` are a caller's error, never a nearby commit. Delete the walk loop."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-point-reads-each-commit-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-task-is-shown-once"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an agent's task (its plan, proofs, cites) is in `@ctx` and drawn again whole on the model's page, so the first request holds it twice. Plan: the task whole is in `@ctx`; the model's page shows one line naming it and what is derived from it (its proofs' states, what its cites resolve to), never the plan's text again (docs/ctx.md#what-the-model-reads-and-what-the-page-renders). Deletes: the page's copy of the plan. Proof, red first: in a seeded agent's first request the plan's text appears once; a recorded request moves by exactly that part."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/the-plan-is-in-the-first-request-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-call-seon-makes-is-known-by-its-function-not-its-text"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-12 (batch 84 red): the calls Seon makes for an agent ((my.agent/describe @ctx), (my.render/page p), (my.render/diff p0 p1)) are written once, by seon.ctx.wire/page-message, as code text, and read back by matching that text: seon.judge/request found the first point's record by the prefix \"(my.render/ctx \", so when the first point became (my.agent/describe @ctx) the judge keyed every pass anew (seon.judge-test/a-pass-scores-each-request-once red). Readers by text, swept with git grep: src: seon.judge/request (fixed to the describe call's text, still text); test: seon.fixtures/two-calls and the scripted model beside it (\"(my.render/\" or \"(my.agent/describe \" prefixes), seon.ctx.wire-test's page-call?, seon.agent-test's page? (twice), and the asserts that compare the code strings. The change: the call has one definition, the function it names as data: page-message records it on the call it writes (the var, beside :code), and every reader asks that (one predicate in seon.ctx.wire, a Seon-made call and which), never the text. Proof: changing the first point's function in one place leaves the judge's key and the fixtures right; a reader that matches the text is red."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-call-seon-makes-is-known-by-its-function}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turn-is-a-component-of-the-request"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the agent's model render, seon.ctx.wire/agent-msgs, places every turn's entities itself (turn-entities: the placed messages, the page pairs, each call and its evals), so any change to the conversation's key set (one eval more) runs it whole again, ~243k ticks at 40 turns, and each of its placed values is a reused component looked up again: seon.render/walk's one eval more costs 420k ticks plus 17k a turn (1.11M at 40; seon.render-cost-test/a-request-made-again-costs-what-moved). The cause: a turn is not a component of the request. Plan: agent-msgs places one call a turn ([#'turn-msgs {} n] with what that turn reads), so one eval more runs its own turn's call and the root's list of calls, each other turn reused by its arguments; the lower bound is that turn's render and the messages put together. Found by Web, 2026-11-12, measuring seon.render/walk. Proofs: that cost proof's estimate made constant in turns (its :per-n gone), and seon.ctx.wire-test's request proofs unchanged."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-cost-test/a-request-made-again-costs-what-moved}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turns-page-message-is-read-from-the-one-walk"
  :seon.task/plan "W-2 (context's, filed from root's notes 2026-11-14). Web's W-4a is on main (c19d3f7e81): the agent's walk is a render/walk value in its state (seon.repl/page-walk), wire/request takes [p db] and reads the answers' sources, and a cold request walks :once, so the second walk below can go. What exists. Each turn's page message is a component the agent's model render places per turn, `[#'page-diff-msgs opts a p0 p1 n]` (wire.clj:490): it diffs the page parts at bases p0 and p1, which come from a second walk, `page-parts-through` (:363): a walk of its own (`parts-walk` :337, rooted at `model-parts` over `page-ctx`, a pull without the conversation's joins), kept in agent/state's one slot and advanced point to point by wire's own `advance` (:348); `pages-from` (:461) shares it across a cold request's pairs. Web's walk (9434c6e308) already draws the same page components each turn, with `:answers` keyed by component and `:ran`. Cause of the duplicate. The model's page parts and the person's page are drawn by two walks over two pulls of one agent; the second exists because the request needed past points' pages and the first walk only drew now. Design. One walk value per owner draws the agent with both outputs. The page parts at a point are that walk's hiccup output of the model-parts components (`model-parts` stays, as a component of the one tree, not a root). A pair's two pages are the walk at p0 and at p1: live, the walk the agent's proc keeps, moved turn to turn (`:moved` from the commit, O(change)); replay (the debug page, the context op), the same walk advanced through the recorded bases in order (`seon.render/walk` with `:moved` between points, as pages-from does today, but the one walk). The diff stays render/page-edits over forms-moved; which parts moved is `:ran`. Deletes (same landing): page-parts-through, parts-walk, wire's advance, page-ctx, pages-from, the ::parts-walk slot in agent/state, page-edits-between's second draw; render.clj's per-pair re-draw if unused after. Keeps: page-message, diff-reply, describe-msgs, page-diff-msgs (its body reads the one walk), editscript diffs (B13's placement stays a config question). Proof, red first. seon.ctx.wire-test: a turn's page message is made from the agent's one walk: no second walk is drawn (a cold request of 3 turns draws its tree once and moves it twice: a call count); the bytes of every turn's messages equal today's (the prefix holds: seon.render-test/the-page-and-the-request-agree and the sent-hash test stay green). Estimate. Live turn: the page parts that moved, O(change), about 1-5M ticks. Cold replay of n turns: one cold draw plus n-1 moves, about 2.5M + n x 1M on session-of's world. src about -180 / +60 in wire.clj. Risk. Replay bytes must equal what was sent (the prefix check, seon.turn/cache-miss): checked by the existing sent-hash test."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/a-turns-page-message-is-read-from-the-one-walk}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-system-message-carries-the-principles"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed from root's notes 2026-11-14 (root's note of 11-13, not built). What exists. The system message is the wiki sections the agent's record names (`:seon.agent/system`, seon.ctx.wire/agent-system-text), else the system prompt page; it is byte-identical across agents (2.5 KB measured), first in every request, so the provider caches it. The carrying. principles.md becomes one of those sections, named by default for every agent (the config's default `:seon.agent/system`), so it is read once per agent at the head of the cached prefix: no per-turn cost after turn 1, shared across agents by the provider's cache (r1 sec. 8: the system message is the one part provably the same). What an agent needs from it, and what it doesn't. An agent works one task: \"boring by construction\" (the one sanctioned place per risk), \"a live REPL platform\" (try small, read your result and your reasoning) and \"raise the alarm\" (a second mechanism, a guard, a wait on a clock: say so in its reply, which reaches its starter) are its business. \"How designs are chosen\" is for sessions building Seon; it costs every agent tokens on every run. So: the page's agent-facing sections go in the system message; the design rules stay a section an agent reads on demand (`(doc \"docs/principles.md#how-designs-are-chosen\")`). Raise the alarm, as data. An agent's alarm is a message to its starter (seon.message) with a schema'd body (``:seon.alarm/rule``, ``:seon.alarm/evidence``), so root and the review task read alarms by query rather than by reading replies. Measure. The system message's tokens before and after, against the 512-1,024-token minimum cacheable prefix (agent-context.md 1.1); the cache-hit ratio per turn must not fall (today 97-98%). Open. Whether alarms need their own schema or are a :seon.task/failure variant; which sections are \"agent-facing\" is the owner's call."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/an-agents-system-message-holds-the-principles-agent-sections}}
